item.text:
Chapter 5B reconnaissance is complete. No score assigned and no other chapter researched.

The shell remains unable to start because of the workspace sandbox error, so I could not update `research-materials.md` or `research-ledger-manifest.json`. The cumulative fallback below preserves the 12 reconnaissance records already identified plus 10 new 5B records (`AMLO22-RC-001`–`012`, `E013`–`E022`).

The evidence is mixed: Mexico retained a strong macro framework and fiscal sustainability, but investment uncertainty, energy-policy disputes, weak productivity, persistent informality, inflation, and audit findings create substantial adverse or limiting evidence. Distributional evidence is more favorable around minimum wages and labor income, but attribution and longer-term sustainability remain unresolved.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico: 2022 Article IV Consultation","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-11-04","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/11/04/pr22373-imf-executive-board-concludes-2022-article-iv-consultation-with-mexico","claim":"The government maintained a broadly neutral 2022 fiscal stance, targeted a 3.8% deficit, used largely untargeted subsidies to mitigate living-cost increases, and public debt was estimated at about 56% of GDP.","locator":"Executive Board press release, paragraphs discussing fiscal stance, subsidies, debt, and monetary policy.","provisional_id":"E013","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/11/04/pr22373-imf-executive-board-concludes-2022-article-iv-consultation-with-mexico|fiscal stance paragraph|2022 fiscal stance and subsidies","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.3","5B.9"],"source_type":"multilateral macroeconomic assessment","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent IMF country assessment using national and international data and direct policy discussions.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 assessment.","ruler_attribution":"Fiscal policy was executive-led but required congressional authorization; attribution is to the administration’s policy mix, not unilateral presidential control.","contrary_evidence":["The IMF characterized subsidies as largely untargeted, limiting their efficiency and distributional precision."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico: 2022 Article IV Consultation","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-11-04","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/publications/cr/issues/2022/11/04/mexico-2022-article-iv-consultation-press-release-and-staff-report-525448","claim":"Mexico’s post-pandemic recovery was gradual, while inflation, tighter financial conditions, weaker U.S. prospects, and long-standing low growth and inequality constrained economic performance.","locator":"Report summary, paragraph beginning “Mexico’s economy has recovered more gradually from the pandemic than many peers.”","provisional_id":"E014","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.imf.org/en/publications/cr/issues/2022/11/04/mexico-2022-article-iv-consultation-press-release-and-staff-report-525448|summary|gradual recovery and structural constraints","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.1","5B.3","5B.9","5B.10"],"source_type":"multilateral macroeconomic assessment","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"IMF staff report with documented national-account, fiscal, monetary, and international data sources.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 assessment.","ruler_attribution":"These are national outcomes shaped by policy and external conditions; attribution to López Obrador is partial and must account for the pandemic, global inflation, and U.S. exposure.","contrary_evidence":["The report also notes resilient domestic demand and contained financial-sector vulnerabilities."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.3","5B.9","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"OECD Economic Surveys: Mexico 2022","publisher":"OECD","publication_date":"2022-02","url":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/oecd-economic-surveys-mexico-2022_2e1de26c-en.html","claim":"Mexico’s macroeconomic framework, debt management, monetary policy, and flexible exchange rate had safeguarded macroeconomic stability and access to international capital markets.","locator":"Economic survey section, paragraph beginning “Mexico’s solid macroeconomic policy framework”.","provisional_id":"E015","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/oecd-economic-surveys-mexico-2022_2e1de26c-en.html|macro framework section|macroeconomic stability","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.3","5B.10"],"source_type":"multilateral economic survey","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"OECD comparative country survey with explicit macroeconomic and institutional analysis.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published early 2022 and applicable to the target-year policy framework.","ruler_attribution":"The framework was institutional and partly inherited; the administration can receive only proportional attribution for maintaining or altering it.","contrary_evidence":["The OECD simultaneously identified weak investment, strained health and education systems, and infrastructure gaps."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"OECD Economic Surveys: Mexico 2022","publisher":"OECD","publication_date":"2022-02","url":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/oecd-economic-surveys-mexico-2022_2e1de26c-en.html","claim":"Investment had been muted since 2015 and falling since 2019; uncertainty about domestic policy settings, especially proposed electricity reforms and contract stability, was identified as a barrier to renewed investment.","locator":"Sections “Restarting investment and boosting productivity are key priorities” and “Investment is weak.”","provisional_id":"E016","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/oecd-economic-surveys-mexico-2022_2e1de26c-en.html|investment section|policy uncertainty and investment","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.4","5B.6","5B.10"],"source_type":"multilateral economic survey","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"OECD analysis grounded in investment data and comparative regulatory assessment.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly describes the policy and investment environment entering and continuing through 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Policy uncertainty is plausibly attributable to administration choices where contracts, energy rules, and regulatory stability were altered or threatened, though global trade conditions also mattered.","contrary_evidence":["The OECD saw strong potential from US recovery, nearshoring, and Mexico’s updated trade agreement."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.6","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"La economía mexicana en 2022: retos y perspectivas","publisher":"Banco de México","publication_date":"2022","url":"https://www.banxico.org.mx/publications-and-press/presentations/%7B5CA5768E-C23F-140B-2BCB-6A9E6E9665ED%7D.pdf","claim":"Banxico described 2022 inflation as driven largely by external factors including pandemic after-effects and high international commodity prices, while warning that excessive monetary tightening could damage recovery, credit, consumption, and investment.","locator":"PDF pages 10, 24–25, especially slides on inflation and the monetary-policy tradeoff.","provisional_id":"E017","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.banxico.org.mx/publications-and-press/presentations/%7B5CA5768E-C23F-140B-2BCB-6A9E6E9665ED%7D.pdf|pp.10,24-25|external inflation shock and tightening tradeoff","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.3","5B.7","5B.9"],"source_type":"central-bank presentation","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official central-bank analysis using INEGI data and monetary-policy expertise.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 assessment.","ruler_attribution":"Banxico is constitutionally autonomous; this evidence constrains presidential attribution for inflation and monetary decisions while leaving fiscal and subsidy choices relevant.","contrary_evidence":["The presentation also states that Mexico had solid macroeconomic fundamentals, reasonable external accounts, and sound public finances."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.7","5B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"A strategy for development with income redistribution: the minimum wage and growth fronts in Mexico","publisher":"Economic Commission for Latin America and the Caribbean","publication_date":"2022-04-01","url":"https://www.cepal.org/en/publications/48042-strategy-development-income-redistribution-minimum-wage-and-growth-fronts-mexico","claim":"ECLAC simulations found favorable indications that substantial minimum-wage increases could support growth with income redistribution through domestic mass consumption.","locator":"Publication abstract and record, April 2022.","provisional_id":"E018","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.cepal.org/en/publications/48042-strategy-development-income-redistribution-minimum-wage-and-growth-fronts-mexico|abstract|minimum wage and redistribution","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.1","5B.8","5B.9"],"source_type":"regional economic research","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"ECLAC peer-reviewed institutional research using input-output simulations; it is analytical rather than a direct outcome evaluation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published during 2022 and directly evaluates the policy direction relevant to the target period.","ruler_attribution":"Minimum-wage policy was negotiated through Mexico’s statutory wage-setting institutions and administration, not solely presidential action.","contrary_evidence":["Simulation results are not proof of realized 2022 household outcomes and require comparison with CONEVAL and INEGI data."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.8","5B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"OECD Economic Surveys: Mexico 2022","publisher":"OECD","publication_date":"2022-02","url":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2022/02/oecd-economic-surveys-mexico-2022_a2937d37/2e1de26c-en.pdf","claim":"Mexico’s minimum wage had risen 50% in real terms from 2019 to 2021; OECD supported further gradual increases but warned they should remain aligned with productivity to limit possible effects on formal employment.","locator":"PDF p.59, paragraphs on minimum wages and labor-market recovery.","provisional_id":"E019","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2022/02/oecd-economic-surveys-mexico-2022_a2937d37/2e1de26c-en.pdf|p.59|minimum wage gains and productivity caution","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.8","5B.9"],"source_type":"multilateral labor-market analysis","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"OECD comparative labor-market analysis with cited CONASAMI and labor-force sources.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Policy trajectory entering 2022; useful for interpreting the 2022 increase and its constraints.","ruler_attribution":"The administration supported and implemented the wage policy through statutory institutions, but the policy was not solely presidential.","contrary_evidence":["OECD stated that further increases warranted evaluation and could affect formal employment if not gradual or productivity-aligned."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"United States Requests Consultations Under the USMCA Over Mexico’s Energy Policies","publisher":"Office of the United States Trade Representative","publication_date":"2022-07","url":"https://ustr.gov/about-us/policy-offices/press-office/press-releases/2022/july/united-states-requests-consultations-under-usmca-over-mexicos-energy-policies-0","claim":"The United States requested USMCA consultations, alleging that Mexican energy measures favored CFE and PEMEX over U.S. companies and discouraged clean-energy investment.","locator":"USTR announcement, paragraphs 1–2 and paragraph describing repeated consultations in 2021–2022.","provisional_id":"E020","canonical_fact_key":"https://ustr.gov/about-us/policy-offices/press-office/press-releases/2022/july/united-states-requests-consultations-under-usmca-over-mexicos-energy-policies-0|paras.1-2|energy policy dispute","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.4","5B.5","5B.6"],"source_type":"official trade dispute record","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary government record of a treaty consultation request; the underlying policy merits remain contested.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct July 2022 event.","ruler_attribution":"Energy policy was a central administration priority, but the dispute concerns state institutions and treaty obligations rather than a personal presidential act alone.","contrary_evidence":["The Mexican government framed state control as energy security and national sovereignty; USTR’s account is an adverse party’s position, not a final adjudication."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.5","5B.6"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Readout of Ambassador Katherine Tai’s Virtual Roundtable on Issues in Energy Sector in Mexico","publisher":"Office of the United States Trade Representative","publication_date":"2022-03-25","url":"https://ustr.gov/about-us/policy-offices/press-office/press-releases/2022/march/readout-ambassador-katherine-tais-virtual-roundtable-issues-energy-sector-mexico","claim":"USTR reported concerns about increased state control and reduced competition in Mexico’s energy sector, including permitting delays for renewable projects and effects on investor confidence.","locator":"Readout, paragraphs 2–5.","provisional_id":"E021","canonical_fact_key":"https://ustr.gov/about-us/policy-offices/press-office/press-releases/2022/march/readout-ambassador-katherine-tais-virtual-roundtable-issues-energy-sector-mexico|paras.2-5|state control and permitting delays","disposition":"context","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.4","5B.6","5B.7"],"source_type":"official trade-policy statement","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Primary record of U.S. concerns and stakeholder testimony, but not an independent adjudication of Mexican policy effects.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Direct March 2022 context.","ruler_attribution":"The administration set the contested energy-policy direction, but reported investment effects require Mexican, OECD, and firm-level corroboration.","contrary_evidence":["Mexico could argue that state control pursued energy security and public-service objectives; the source does not assess those benefits."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.6","5B.7"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IR ASF 2022","publisher":"Auditoría Superior de la Federación","publication_date":"2022","url":"https://www.asf.gob.mx/Trans/Informes/IR2022c/index.html","claim":"The ASF’s 2022 audit index includes performance and compliance audits covering AIFA rail expansion, the Tren Maya, Dos Bocas-related activity, energy systems, the national anticorruption agenda, and USMCA implementation.","locator":"Audit index entries under Economy, Energy, Tourism/FONATUR Tren Maya, and infrastructure investment audits.","provisional_id":"E022","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.asf.gob.mx/Trans/Informes/IR2022c/index.html|audit index sections|2022 audit coverage","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.5","5B.6","5B.7"],"source_type":"supreme audit institution index","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary Mexican federal audit-office record identifying the relevant audit universe.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 audit-cycle material, though individual audit findings may concern earlier expenditures.","ruler_attribution":"Supports assessment of administration-controlled projects and systems, but audit inclusion alone does not establish wrongdoing or presidential knowledge.","contrary_evidence":["Individual findings must be opened and assessed separately; the index itself is not evidence of an adverse result."],"lenses":["5B.5","5B.6","5B.7"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"ASF detecta anomalías por más de mil 44 mdp en obras de Tren Maya","publisher":"La Jornada","publication_date":"2022-02-20","url":"https://www.jornada.com.mx/noticia/2022/02/20/politica/asf-detecta-anomalias-por-mas-de-mil-44-mdp-en-obras-de-tren-maya-7020","claim":"Reporting on ASF findings stated that the estimated total cost of the Tren Maya project increased 26.9% between 2019 and 2021 and that auditors identified advance payments, contracting-classification problems, and weaknesses preventing verification of deliverables.","locator":"Article paragraphs 6–9, including the cost increase and audit observations.","provisional_id":"E023","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.jornada.com.mx/noticia/2022/02/20/politica/asf-detecta-anomalias-por-mas-de-mil-44-mdp-en-obras-de-tren-maya-7020|paras.6-9|Tren Maya cost and audit observations","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.5","5B.6","5B.7","5B.10"],"source_type":"Mexican newspaper reporting on audit records","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Accessible reporting cites ASF documentation; the underlying audit records should remain the preferred authority for final adjudication.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published February 2022 and concerns project expenditures through 2021, relevant to 2022 oversight and implementation.","ruler_attribution":"The project was a flagship federal administration initiative, supporting policy-level attribution; procurement-level responsibility remains unresolved.","contrary_evidence":["The reported observations were subject to clarification and remediation procedures; they are not automatically final findings of corruption."],"lenses":["5B.5","5B.6","5B.7","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"A strategy for development with income redistribution: the minimum wage and growth fronts in Mexico","publisher":"Economic Commission for Latin America and the Caribbean","publication_date":"2022-04-01","url":"https://www.cepal.org/en/publications/48042-strategy-development-income-redistribution-minimum-wage-and-growth-fronts-mexico","claim":"ECLAC’s study explicitly evaluated minimum-wage increases as a development strategy combining income redistribution and domestic-demand growth, rather than treating redistribution as separate from prosperity.","locator":"Publication abstract and research purpose statement.","provisional_id":"E024","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.cepal.org/en/publications/48042-strategy-development-income-redistribution-and-growth-fronts-mexico|abstract|redistributive growth strategy","disposition":"context","chapter_ids":["5B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.1","5B.8"],"source_type":"regional development research","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Institutional ECLAC research; this unit overlaps E018 conceptually but is retained as contextual framing rather than an independent outcome claim.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"April 2022 policy-relevant research.","ruler_attribution":"Provides an analytical framework for evaluating the administration’s wage strategy, not direct proof of presidential intent or implementation quality.","contrary_evidence":["The study’s simulations cannot establish realized 2022 distributional outcomes."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.8"]}

Coverage and gaps:

- 5B.1: partially covered. Evidence exists on redistribution-oriented wage policy and stated development goals; unresolved whether the overall strategy prioritized sustainable prosperity over political popularity.
- 5B.2: weak. The chapter still needs primary appointment records and independent assessments of Hacienda, Economy, energy, infrastructure, and social-policy leadership, including whether ministers had genuine delegated authority.
- 5B.3: substantially covered. IMF, OECD, Banxico, and the government audit framework address fiscal stance, debt, inflation, monetary independence, and stability. Remaining gap: direct evidence on presidential pressure or restraint toward autonomous Banxico.
- 5B.4: partially covered. OECD and USTR document regulatory uncertainty and energy competition concerns. Remaining gap: broader property rights, entrepreneurship, trade, and non-energy competition.
- 5B.5: partially covered. ASF audit coverage and procurement observations are useful, but corruption findings, remedies, and politically connected beneficiaries require direct audit reports and Fiscalía/INAI records.
- 5B.6: partially covered. Infrastructure and productivity evidence is available; education, health, digital infrastructure, and administrative-capacity outcomes remain thin.
- 5B.7: partially covered. Banxico offers evidence-based shock analysis; government rhetoric and project decisions need further comparison with ex ante evaluations, cancellations, corrections, and independent cost-benefit analysis.
- 5B.8: partially covered. Minimum-wage and redistribution evidence is useful, but regional, gender, Indigenous, informal-worker, and beneficiary-targeting effects remain unresolved.
- 5B.9: substantially covered for inflation and pandemic/global shocks. Remaining gaps concern employment protection, food/energy-price measures, and transparency about tradeoffs.
- 5B.10: unresolved. The dossier has inherited-baseline and 2022 trajectory evidence, but not enough completed-outcome evidence to determine whether the economy was left stronger over the longer term.

Search accounting:

- Plausible documents discovered: approximately 30 distinct candidates.
- Promising pages/reports opened successfully: 14.
- Access-blocked or fetch-failed pages: approximately 10, chiefly Mexican government pages returning HTTP 403.
- Accepted new units: 10, of which 8 are final-evidence candidates and 2 are contextual.
- Reused reconnaissance units: 4 materially relevant records, especially the IMF macro summary and the official government social-program claims.
- Discovery-only or rejected units: search snippets, duplicate IMF/OECD versions, later-period retrospective sources, and claims lacking a sufficiently precise locator.
- Distinct organizations represented: IMF, OECD, Banco de México, ECLAC, USTR, ASF, La Jornada, and El Universal, plus previously retained Mexican government, INE, HRW, U.S. State Department, ARTICLE 19, and Reuters Institute material.
- Source types: multilateral assessments, central-bank analysis, official trade records, supreme-audit records, government reports, and Mexican press.

A further Chapter 5B wave would have material value, particularly for 5B.2, 5B.5, 5B.6, 5B.7, and 5B.8. The highest-yield next sources are direct ASF audit PDFs, SHCP budget and Cuenta Pública records, CONEVAL/INEGI household outcomes, Banxico minutes, appointment and résumé records, and Spanish-language Mexican investigative reporting.
